<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dar es Salaam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dar es Salaam is the largest city and chief port of Tanzania, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast. Founded in the 1860s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Majid of Zanzibar, it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> in 1891 and the starting point of the Central Railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. It remained the capital under British rule and after independence, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodoma">Dodoma</a> has since been designated the official capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam.jpg" alt="Dar es Salaam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Joseph%27s_Catholic_Cathedral_(34895613805).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dar es Salaam lies at 6°48' S, 39°17' E, on a natural harbour on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, with sandy beaches in places. The climate is tropical savanna, hot and humid for much of the year, with about 1,150 millimetres of rain annually and two rainy seasons, the long rains in April and May and the short rains in November and December. The city is co-extensive with the Dar es Salaam Region, which has five districts: Kinondoni, Ilala, Ubungo, Temeke and Kigamboni.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 19th century Mzizima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> for 'healthy town', was a coastal fishing village on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> routes. In 1865 or 1866 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majid-bin-said">Majid bin Said of Zanzibar</a> began building a new city close to it and named it Dar es Salaam, commonly translated from Arabic as 'abode of peace'. The town declined after Majid's death in 1870.</p><p>It revived in 1887, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa-company">German East Africa Company</a> established a station there. Its growth then followed its role as the administrative and commercial centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, and industrial expansion after the building of the Central Railway Line in the early 1900s. It continued as the main administrative and commercial centre of Tanganyika and then of Tanzania. In 1974 a decision was made to move the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodoma">Dodoma</a>, a move officially completed in 1996.</p><p>In 1949 the town became a municipality, with nominated town councillors who elected a mayor, according to the historian John Iliffe. The city's first radio station began operating in the early 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dar es Salaam is the largest city and financial centre of Tanzania, with a population of more than 7 million. About 70 per cent of its people are Muslim, and it has many churches, mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples. The Port of Dar es Salaam handles about 90 per cent of the country's cargo, and the Central Line runs west from the city to Kigoma. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzania-zambia-railway">TAZARA</a> railway, built in the late 1960s and early 1970s, links it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p><p>The Tingatinga painting style originates in the city. It has the National Museum, which shows bones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranthropus">Paranthropus</a> boisei found by Louis Leakey at Olduvai, and the University of Dar es Salaam, the oldest public university in Tanzania.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dar es Salaam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German rule was established quickly over Bagamoyo, <b>Dar es Salaam</b> and Kilwa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kilwa Kisiwani is a small island off the southern coast of present-day Tanzania, about 300 kilometres south of <b>Dar es Salaam</b>, with a sheltered harbour well placed for ships sailing on the monsoon winds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Majid (1856–1870) began building <b>Dar es Salaam</b> on the mainland in the 1860s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Since liberalisation in the mid-1980s the economy has grown steadily, led by mining, tourism around Mount Kilimanjaro, the Serengeti and Zanzibar, and the port of <b>Dar es Salaam</b>, the largest city and an outlet for several landlocked neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…standard language has been developed by institutions such as the Institute of Kiswahili Research at the University of <b>Dar es Salaam</b>, founded in 1964, and Tanzania’s National Swahili Council, founded in 1967, which create new terms for science, technology and government, often from Bantu roots as well as…</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sixth Pan-African Congress, the first held in Africa, met in <b>Dar es Salaam</b> in 1974, and in 2002 the African Union replaced the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
